(ns kotoba.phone.ports
  "Host-injected ports for a numbering and outbound-calling host.

  This namespace defines the protocols; the host (a governed actor) supplies
  concrete implementations backed by a real numbering plane, a real MNP
  clearinghouse, or fixtures offline. Same posture as kotoba-lang/koe's voice
  ports and kotoba.esim.ports, which this file is modeled on.

  No SDK, no endpoint, no carrier credential and no rate card lives here -- only
  the shapes a host is built from. An implementation that hardcodes a
  clearinghouse address or an operator id has put deployment configuration in a
  portable library and should carry it in the host instead.

  ## Why these are three protocols and not one

  Holding a port must not get you an authority you were not given. The split is
  the same discipline kotoba.esim.ports applies by separating IOwnershipTransfer
  from IProfileProvisioning, and it matters more here, not less:

    INumberInventory   giving somebody a number
    INumberPorting     moving a number between OPERATORS  <- the takeover path
    IOutboundCall      spending money and reaching people

  A host that provisions numbers has no business being able to port one out
  merely because it holds a port, and a host that places calls has no business
  reassigning numbers. An implementation is free to implement all three; a
  CALLER is not free to reach all three by holding one.

  ## Relationship to koe.ports/ITelephony

  koe's ITelephony owns a voice SESSION once it exists: `answer`, `say`,
  `hangup`. IOutboundCall owns only the act of CREATING one outbound, and hands
  back a session handle of the same shape. Nothing here duplicates `say` or
  `hangup` -- a call is a call once it is up, whoever started it, and forking the
  session model would give the same conversation two incompatible descriptions.

  Portable (.cljc) across JVM / ClojureScript / SCI / GraalVM."
  (:refer-clojure :exclude [find]))

(defprotocol INumberInventory
  "The numbering plane: what this operator holds and what each number is doing.

  Implementations are expected to report refusal as data rather than throw,
  matching kotoba.phone.lifecycle/apply-event. Reachability is the caller's
  responsibility: an implementation is entitled to assume the lifecycle already
  admitted the operation, and entitled to refuse again if its own view
  disagrees."
  (blocks [this]
    "Return the numbering blocks this operator holds, as kotoba.phone.numbering/block records.")
  (find [this msisdn]
    "Return the number record for msisdn, or nil when this operator has none.")
  (reserve [this msisdn]
    "Take msisdn out of the pool without naming a holder. Returns the updated
     record or a refusal map.")
  (assign [this msisdn subject]
    "Name the holder of a reserved number.")
  (release [this msisdn]
    "Give a number up. It does NOT return to the pool here -- see
     kotoba.phone.lifecycle: the path back runs through quarantine, and an
     implementation that shortcuts it has re-issued somebody's second factor."))

(defprotocol INumberPorting
  "Moving a number between operators.

  Deliberately a separate protocol rather than operations on INumberInventory: a
  port-out is the primary account-takeover path on the number plane, so it is a
  distinct authority a host can decline to implement or gate far more strictly,
  and a caller cannot reach it by holding an inventory port (ADR-2607300300 D6,
  applied to numbering)."
  (propose-port-out [this request]
    "Record a kotoba.phone.porting/port-request as proposed. An implementation
     must NOT actuate here -- a port-out always requires human escalation.")
  (propose-port-in [this request]
    "Record an inbound port request as proposed.")
  (port-status [this msisdn]
    "Return the pending or last-recorded port for msisdn, or nil."))

(defprotocol IOutboundCall
  "Placing a call.

  `originate` is the only operation: once a call is up it is a koe voice session
  and koe.ports/ITelephony owns it. An implementation must not treat a
  successful originate as permission to place another -- one admission, one
  call."
  (originate [this request]
    "Place an outbound call described by a kotoba.phone.origination request.
     Returns a session handle for koe.ports/ITelephony, or a refusal map.")
  (rate [this destination]
    "Return the cost per minute in integer MINOR units for this destination, or
     nil when this host cannot price it. nil must not be read as free -- see
     kotoba.phone.origination/cost-issues, where an unknown rate refuses."))
